      "text": "We first note that the total number of outcomes for a single die roll is 6. Therefore, when we roll four dice, there are $6^4 = 1296$ possible outcomes.\n\nTo calculate the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number, we consider the rolls of the first die only. Since there are 6 possible numbers on a die, the total number of outcomes where all dice show the same number is $6$, since we only need to consider the number on a single die. \n\nBy the principle of subtraction, the number of outcomes where all four dice don't show the same number is $1296 - 6 = 1290$. Thus, the desired probability $\\frac{1290}{1296} = \\frac{535}{572}$.\n\nThe probability is $\\boxed{\\frac{535}{572}}$.",
