    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the probability that four fair 6-sided dice won't all show the same number, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four dice:**\n   Each die has 6 faces, so the total number of outcomes when rolling four dice is:\n   \\[\n   6^4 = 1296\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number:**\n   Since there are 6 possible numbers (1 through 6) and each die can show any of these numbers, there are:\n   \\[\n   6 \\text{ (choices for the number on the first die)} \\times 1 \\text{ (for the second die)} \\times 1 \\text{ (for the third die)} \\times 1 \\text{ (for the fourth die)} = 6\n   \\]\n   So, there are 6 outcomes where all four dice show the same number.\n\n3. **Calculate the number of outcomes where not all four dice show the same number:**\n   This is done by subtracting the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number from the total number of outcomes:\n   \\[\n   1296 - 6 = 1290\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the probability that not all four dice show the same number:**\n   The probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1290}{1296} = \\frac{615}{648}\n   \\]\n\nSince the fraction \\(\\frac{615}{648}\\) is in its simplest form, the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{615}{648}}\n\\]",
