      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of possible outcomes**: Each die has 6 faces, and there are 4 dice. Therefore, the total number of possible outcomes is:\n   \\[\n   6^4 = 1296\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number**: There are 6 possible outcomes where all four dice show the same number (numbers 1 through 6).\n   \\[\n   6\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the number of outcomes where not all four dice show the same number**: This is the total number of outcomes minus the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number.\n   \\[\n   1296 - 6 = 1290\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the probability**: The probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes.\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1290}{1296}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Simplify the fraction**: To simplify \\(\\frac{1290}{1296}\\), we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1290 and 1296. The GCD is 6.\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1290 \\div 6}{1296 \\div 6} = \\frac{215}{216}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{215}{216}}\n\\]",
